Restart input after an edit crosses an open composition.

Pasting at the end of a composing word moves the caret but leaves Chromium's
composition range at the offsets from before the paste. The next keyboard
update replaces that old range and repeats the inserted text. On API 33 with
Gboard 12.4, the editor's context-menu Paste and hardware Ctrl+V changed
helloab into helloabXYZXYZ with WebView 134 and 157. On API 29 with Gboard 8.0.4
and WebView 134, moving the buffer to the caret's line during the paste left
an old range from another line; two recompositions repeated the word again.

Keep whether a composition was open before the first rewrite alongside the
text and selection already saved for the pending batch. At the existing
microtask, restart input if the text changed and either the selection did
not move or that composition was open and is still open. Composition start
and end track the mark, and a separate blur listener clears it even while
the editor's focus tracker is paused. An empty commit can end the native
composition without a compositionend; its stale mark lasts until blur or
end and can cost one extra restart on a later non-IME edit.

The Android-only gate and the paused focus tracker stay in place. The origin
flags exclude the input method's own synchronous work, including typing and
plain Enter. Later work caused by typing is not excluded: format-on-type,
off by default at this pin, can end an unconverted word when its delayed
edit changes the buffer. Coalescing gives one restart per pending batch;
a further edit made by composition-end can queue another. A page guard that
already ended the composition needs no second restart for the new reason.

From the source, this also reaches Undo and Redo, a snippet, an extension's
edit, and Enter accepting a suggestion if composition is still open when
the command changes the buffer. That Enter route was not exercised by the
measured suggestion-list sequences: Gboard 12.4 committed alp and a newline
in one textupdate, while Gboard 8.0.4 ended composition before Enter accepted
alpha1. A command such as Go to Line, Find or a jump also reaches the rule
when moving the caret to another line moves the buffer and changes its text.
A move within the line that changes no buffer text remains uncovered. These
command routes are read from the source, not established by the paste runs.
The restart commits the word as typed and can refresh the suggestion strip.

Ending the composition runs the editor's existing composition-end rules now.
With a word reopened for composition, a single pasted opening bracket is
closed as if typed; a quote is too where its setting permits closing there.
For the old-range-update-first ordering recorded on Gboard 12.4, a replay
of a lone ( produces helloab(( before this change and helloab() after; the
former is a replay result, not a device measurement of that opener.
On API 29 with Gboard 8.0.4 and WebView 134, the opener was already closed
without this change: the keyboard ended composition 264 ms after paste,
the editor added ), and 0024 restarted input once. On the built editor with
Gboard 12.4 and WebView 134, pasting ( instead produced helloab() immediately,
then helloab(x) after x, with two editor restarts and no further restart.
The added closer keeps the caret where it was, so the old unchanged-selection
rule causes the second restart. A longer paste and a lone non-opener add no
such edit. In the constructed case where composition started over selected
helloab, typing replaced it with x, and x was selected again and replaced by
a pasted (, the end rules restore (helloab), selecting helloab. That is the
same result as before, reached sooner; this sequence is not a device result.

A mouse or an injected pointer with an empty pointerType can rewrite the
buffer on pointerdown. If the keyboard reopens a composition before the later
gesture, the page guard can restart it again there. A finger's touch pointer
is ignored by that mouse path, so this is not an extra restart per finger tap.
The change does not cover stale keyboard text carried across a focus move
where the editor rewrites no buffer.

On the built editor on API 33 with Gboard 12.4 and WebView 134, context-menu
Paste and hardware Ctrl+V left helloabXYZ, with one editor restart each;
Edit > Paste left the same text with none, composition having ended when
the menu opened. A built-editor context-menu paste on API 29 with Gboard
8.0.4 and WebView 134 also left helloabXYZ with one editor restart. The
keyboard's next update in each composing paste used the new 0..10 range.
The combined editor also kept the four 0024 Enter flows and key-row Tab
acceptance on Gboard 12.4: Enter added no editor restart and Tab kept one
guard restart. With 0047 and 0048 also built, on an API 34 emulator with
Gboard 12.4 and WebView 157, a paste over underlined helloab left helloabXYZ
in three runs. This still relies on the keyboard reading the final buffer
after restart; it does not discard a stale update sent afterward. On a later
build with this change, on an API 36 emulator with Gboard 15.9 and WebView
134, a key-row {} or Tab with a Korean or Japanese word composing met the
page guard first in all twelve runs: the guard ended the composition, the
word stayed, and this change did not restart input. A restart over
unconverted Japanese, Korean or Chinese input, and delayed format-on-type,
remain device checks.

